1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,421
I bought a time machine for five dollars.

2
00:00:02,761 --> 00:00:05,723
Ten seconds later, I was standing on the Titanic.

3
00:00:06,063 --> 00:00:07,687
Worst purchase of my life!

4
00:00:12,687 --> 00:00:14,963
It was a sunny Sunday at the flea market.

5
00:00:15,303 --> 00:00:18,894
Old lamps, broken toys, a lot of very suspicious socks.

6
00:00:19,234 --> 00:00:20,406
And then I saw it.

7
00:00:20,746 --> 00:00:25,635
A dusty brass machine with dials, buttons, and a little sign that said: Time machine.

8
00:00:25,975 --> 00:00:27,112
Five dollars.

9
00:00:27,452 --> 00:00:28,574
No refunds.

10
00:00:28,974 --> 00:00:32,889
The old man selling it winked at me and said, Don't touch the red lever.

11
00:00:33,229 --> 00:00:37,053
So, obviously, I paid him, carried it home, and touched the red lever.

12
00:00:37,453 --> 00:00:38,757
The machine rattled.

13
00:00:39,097 --> 00:00:40,323
The dials spun.

14
00:00:40,663 --> 00:00:42,842
My room turned into a tunnel of light.

15
00:00:43,182 --> 00:00:46,825
And a little screen blinked: April the fourteenth, nineteen twelve.

16
00:00:47,925 --> 00:00:50,963
I landed on a wooden deck, under a bright blue sky.

17
00:00:51,303 --> 00:00:52,565
Huge chimneys.

18
00:00:52,905 --> 00:00:54,850
Fancy people in long coats.

19
00:00:55,190 --> 00:00:57,834
A ship so big it looked like a floating city.

20
00:00:58,233 --> 00:00:59,875
And wow, was it fancy.

21
00:01:00,215 --> 00:01:02,401
A grand staircase with a glass dome.

22
00:01:02,741 --> 00:01:04,171
Crystal chandeliers.

23
00:01:04,511 --> 00:01:08,893
A gym, a swimming pool, and a restaurant where the menu had eleven courses.

24
00:01:09,233 --> 00:01:13,167
I ate four bread rolls before anyone noticed I wasn't a passenger.

25
00:01:13,567 --> 00:01:15,142
Then I picked up a menu.

26
00:01:15,482 --> 00:01:19,685
On the top, in fancy gold letters, it said: R M S Titanic.

27
00:01:20,025 --> 00:01:21,211
I read it once.

28
00:01:21,551 --> 00:01:22,832
I read it twice.

29
00:01:23,171 --> 00:01:25,445
And my brain made a very small noise.

30
00:01:26,045 --> 00:01:27,240
Here's what I knew.

31
00:01:27,580 --> 00:01:32,650
The Titanic left England on April the tenth, nineteen twelve, heading for New York.

32
00:01:32,990 --> 00:01:35,616
Newspapers called it practically unsinkable.

33
00:01:35,956 --> 00:01:40,472
And on the night of April the fourteenth, it hit an iceberg in the North Atlantic.

34
00:01:40,812 --> 00:01:42,015
That was tonight.

35
00:01:42,615 --> 00:01:44,655
So I did what any hero would do.

36
00:01:44,995 --> 00:01:47,611
I jumped on a table and shouted: Iceberg!

37
00:01:47,951 --> 00:01:51,274
A waiter nodded politely, and brought me a glass of water.

38
00:01:51,614 --> 00:01:52,470
With ice.

39
00:01:52,870 --> 00:01:54,037
I tried again.

40
00:01:54,377 --> 00:01:57,100
Ladies and gentlemen, this ship is going to sink!

41
00:01:57,440 --> 00:02:00,847
A lady in a giant hat smiled and said: Oh, yes, dear.

42
00:02:01,187 --> 00:02:03,332
The sinks in first class are lovely.

43
00:02:03,731 --> 00:02:05,629
I grabbed a man in a top hat.

44
00:02:05,969 --> 00:02:07,454
There aren't enough lifeboats!

45
00:02:07,794 --> 00:02:08,628
He laughed.

46
00:02:08,968 --> 00:02:11,147
Young man, this ship is unsinkable.

47
00:02:11,487 --> 00:02:13,991
The lifeboats are basically decorations.

48
00:02:14,391 --> 00:02:15,954
And here's the scary part.

49
00:02:16,294 --> 00:02:18,508
He was half right about the decorations.

50
00:02:18,848 --> 00:02:23,553
The Titanic had twenty lifeboats, with room for about eleven hundred people.

51
00:02:23,893 --> 00:02:26,791
But there were around twenty two hundred people on board.

52
00:02:27,292 --> 00:02:30,118
So I drew the iceberg on a napkin to explain.

53
00:02:30,458 --> 00:02:33,532
Everyone clapped, and someone ordered two of them for dessert.

54
00:02:34,032 --> 00:02:34,906
Okay.

55
00:02:35,246 --> 00:02:36,212
New plan.

56
00:02:36,552 --> 00:02:37,852
Talk to the captain.

57
00:02:38,192 --> 00:02:43,106
I found Captain Edward Smith on the bridge, with a white beard and a very serious hat.

58
00:02:43,506 --> 00:02:45,917
Captain, I said, you're going to hit an iceberg!

59
00:02:46,257 --> 00:02:47,880
He raised one eyebrow.

60
00:02:48,220 --> 00:02:48,897
When?

61
00:02:49,237 --> 00:02:50,257
Umm, tonight.

62
00:02:50,597 --> 00:02:51,768
Around midnight.

63
00:02:52,108 --> 00:02:52,740
Ish.

64
00:02:53,080 --> 00:02:53,731
Where?

65
00:02:54,071 --> 00:02:54,876
Somewhere...

66
00:02:55,216 --> 00:02:55,846
wet.

67
00:02:56,246 --> 00:02:57,651
And how do you know this?

68
00:02:57,991 --> 00:02:58,987
I panicked.

69
00:02:59,327 --> 00:03:00,640
I saw it in a movie!

70
00:03:00,980 --> 00:03:01,954
He frowned.

71
00:03:02,294 --> 00:03:03,504
What is a movie?

72
00:03:03,843 --> 00:03:09,830
That was when I realized movies with sound didn't exist yet, and I was losing this conversation.

73
00:03:10,230 --> 00:03:12,473
But I remembered one real detail.

74
00:03:12,813 --> 00:03:15,896
The lookouts in the crow's nest didn't have binoculars.

75
00:03:16,236 --> 00:03:19,388
The key to their locker had been left behind in England.

76
00:03:19,728 --> 00:03:22,625
So I ran off to find some binoculars myself.

77
00:03:23,125 --> 00:03:24,643
That ship was enormous.

78
00:03:24,983 --> 00:03:29,763
I found a squash court, a mail room, a very angry cook, and three thousand stairs.

79
00:03:30,103 --> 00:03:32,170
I did not find any binoculars.

80
00:03:32,670 --> 00:03:35,149
By the time I climbed back up, it was night.

81
00:03:35,489 --> 00:03:36,875
The air was freezing.

82
00:03:37,215 --> 00:03:39,461
The sea was flat and black, like glass.

83
00:03:39,801 --> 00:03:40,708
No moon.

84
00:03:41,048 --> 00:03:42,055
No waves.

85
00:03:42,395 --> 00:03:43,498
Just stars.

86
00:03:43,838 --> 00:03:44,768
Beautiful.

87
00:03:45,108 --> 00:03:46,293
And terrifying.

88
00:03:46,893 --> 00:03:52,730
I climbed to the crow's nest with a pair of opera glasses I borrowed from a very confused lady.

89
00:03:53,070 --> 00:03:55,136
The lookout squinted into the dark.

90
00:03:55,476 --> 00:03:56,865
Then he grabbed the bell.

91
00:03:57,205 --> 00:03:58,275
Three rings.

92
00:03:58,615 --> 00:03:59,933
Iceberg, right ahead!

93
00:04:00,333 --> 00:04:04,052
At eleven forty that night, the Titanic scraped along the iceberg.

94
00:04:04,392 --> 00:04:06,011
It didn't feel like a crash.

95
00:04:06,351 --> 00:04:08,258
It felt like a long, slow shiver.

96
00:04:08,598 --> 00:04:12,132
Upstairs, some passengers played with chunks of ice on the deck.

97
00:04:14,232 --> 00:04:15,857
And that's when I understood.

98
00:04:16,197 --> 00:04:19,511
Changing history is not like changing a TV channel.

99
00:04:19,851 --> 00:04:22,093
I knew the ending, but I couldn't stop it.

100
00:04:22,433 --> 00:04:24,029
All I could do was help.

101
00:04:24,829 --> 00:04:28,143
So I stopped trying to be a hero, and started being useful.

102
00:04:28,483 --> 00:04:32,116
I knocked on doors and told people to put on their life jackets.

103
00:04:32,456 --> 00:04:35,026
I showed families the way up to the boat deck.

104
00:04:35,426 --> 00:04:40,932
The first lifeboats left half empty, because many people still didn't believe the ship could sink.

105
00:04:41,272 --> 00:04:43,693
So I kept saying the only thing that mattered.

106
00:04:44,033 --> 00:04:45,348
Every seat counts.

107
00:04:45,688 --> 00:04:47,096
Please, get in the boat.

108
00:04:47,496 --> 00:04:50,170
I helped a little girl find her mother in the crowd.

109
00:04:50,510 --> 00:04:53,838
I gave my coat to an old man who was shaking from the cold.

110
00:04:54,178 --> 00:04:58,105
And all around us, the ship's band kept playing, to keep everyone calm.

111
00:04:58,705 --> 00:05:02,619
The Titanic sank at two twenty in the morning, on April the fifteenth.

112
00:05:02,959 --> 00:05:05,681
More than fifteen hundred people lost their lives.

113
00:05:06,021 --> 00:05:10,509
The ship Carpathia rescued about seven hundred survivors from the lifeboats.

114
00:05:10,849 --> 00:05:15,678
It's one of the saddest nights in history, and it changed the rules of the sea forever.

115
00:05:16,019 --> 00:05:20,846
After the Titanic, every ship had to carry enough lifeboats for everyone on board.

116
00:05:23,646 --> 00:05:28,811
Sitting in a lifeboat, wrapped in a blanket, I felt the machine in my pocket start to buzz.

117
00:05:29,151 --> 00:05:32,065
The dials spun, the light came back, and I was home.

118
00:05:32,405 --> 00:05:34,152
Sitting on my bedroom floor.

119
00:05:34,492 --> 00:05:35,519
Soaking wet.

120
00:05:36,019 --> 00:05:38,337
I looked at the machine, and made a promise.

121
00:05:38,677 --> 00:05:40,152
No more time travel.

122
00:05:40,492 --> 00:05:41,560
Never again.

123
00:05:41,900 --> 00:05:45,128
I'm putting you in the closet, and I'm never touching you again.

124
00:05:45,528 --> 00:05:50,127
And then I tripped over my backpack, fell on the machine, and heard a very small click.

125
00:05:50,467 --> 00:05:51,757
The screen lit up.

126
00:05:52,097 --> 00:05:53,052
Pompeii.

127
00:05:53,392 --> 00:05:54,847
A D seventy nine.

128
00:05:55,647 --> 00:05:56,911
ARE YOU KIDDING ME?!

129
00:05:58,911 --> 00:06:01,889
Subscribe to History World to see what happens next!
